  "final_message": "<think>No browser available. I could install playwright but that's heavy and may not have network access for browser binaries (the sandbox has no other network access). Let me try a lighter approach: render the React app in Node using react-dom/server? That would catch runtime errors in the initial render (though effects don't run in SSR).\n\nBetter approach: use jsdom + react-dom/client to actually mount the app in a simulated DOM with a mocked fetch, then assert on the rendered output. jsdom is an npm package; installing it is possible. Let me do that as a temporary dev check (not adding to package.json permanently... actually I could add it as a devDependency and write a small test script; but keeping the repo clean is better).\n\nLet me install jsdom temporarily in a separate directory inside the workspace (e.g. /workspace/.tmp-jest) so it doesn't pollute the frontend package.json... but npm install in a subdirectory would create node_modules there. It's temporary; I'll delete it after.\n\nActually simpler: install jsdom into the frontend's node_modules temporarily (npm install --no-save jsdom) and write a test script under .tmp. Then remove.\n\nLet me think about whether it's worth it. The app is fairly simple; the main risks are runtime errors in components. A jsdom smoke test would catch typos/undefined access. I think it's worth doing \u2014 it validates the whole frontend flow (register, list, add book, review).\n\nPlan for the smoke test:\n1. Install jsdom (--no-save).\n2. Use vite's build output? The built bundle is an ES module referencing `/assets/...`. In Node with jsdom, I can load the built JS by creating a jsdom window, setting globals, and importing the bundle file... The bundle is an ES module with no imports (all bundled). It expects `document.getElementById(\"root\")`.\n\nApproach: use jsdom to create a DOM with <div id=\"root\">, set global.window/document/navigator/etc., then dynamically import the built ESM file (file:// URL). ESM import in Node works with `import(pathToFileURL(file))`. The bundle may use `import.meta` etc. \u2014 fine.\n\nThen mock `fetch` to talk to the real backend at 127.0.0.1:8000? jsdom's fetch isn't implemented; Node 24 has global fetch, but the bundle will call `fetch` \u2014 if I set `globalThis.fetch` to Node's fetch with an absolute-URL wrapper (resolving relative paths against http://127.0.0.1:8000), that works nicely and tests the real backend!\n\nAlso `window.localStorage` \u2014 jsdom supports localStorage.\n\n`window.scrollTo` \u2014 jsdom throws \"not implemented\" errors; I can stub it.\n\nLet me do this. It's a good verification.\n\nSteps:\n- npm i --no-save jsdom (in frontend)\n- write /workspace/frontend/.tmp_smoke.mjs:\n  ```js\n  import { JSDOM } from \"jsdom\";\n  const dom = new JSDOM(`<!DOCTYPE html><html><body><div id=\"root\"></div></body></html>`, { url: \"http://localhost/\" });\n  global.window = dom.window; global.document = dom.window.document; global.navigator = dom.window.navigator;\n  global.localStorage = dom.window.localStorage;\n  global.HTMLElement = dom.window.HTMLElement; ...\n  dom.window.scrollTo = () => {};\n  const realFetch = globalThis.fetch;\n  globalThis.fetch = (input, init) => realFetch(new URL(String(input), \"http://127.0.0.1:8000\"), init);\n  dom.window.fetch = globalThis.fetch;\n  await import(pathToFileURL(\"./dist/assets/index-XXX.js\"));\n  ```\n  Wait: the bundle uses `window.fetch`? No, it uses the global `fetch` \u2192 in the module scope, `fetch` resolves to globalThis.fetch. Since I set globalThis.fetch, fine. But jsdom's window has its own fetch? jsdom doesn't implement fetch, so `window.fetch` is undefined; the bundle referencing bare `fetch` will look up the global scope \u2192 globalThis.fetch (which I set). Good.\n\n- Then wait a tick and check document.body.innerHTML contains expected content.\n- Simulate interactions: click buttons, fill inputs, dispatch events. React 19 with jsdom: need `act`? Without react-dom/test-utils, I can dispatch events and await a microtask flush. React's event system attaches listeners at the root container, so dispatching a native \"click\" event with bubbles:true on a button should trigger React's onClick.\n\nThis is doable but fiddly. Let me do a moderate version:\n1. Mount the app, check the header renders \"Book Library\" and the books table.\n2. Fill the search input and check filtering (maybe skip).\n3. Register a user via the API directly (fetch), set the token in localStorage, remount \u2192 check \"Signed in as\".\n4. Click \"Add book\" link \u2192 navigate \u2192 fill the form \u2192 submit \u2192 check the book appears.\n\nLet me try. If it becomes too flaky, I'll settle for a mount + render check plus manual code review.\n\nLet me install jsdom.</think>\n\n",
